  "analysis": "We need to compute the total length of the walls that remain unilluminated in a rectilinear polygon room with one lamp inside. Walls reflect light at most once. Implementing an exact visibility-with-reflection algorithm is complex, so I propose a robust, though approximate, approach suitable for small N (<=20) and integer coordinates: discretize the boundary into many sample points, determine if each sample point is illuminated directly or via exactly one reflection, and then accumulate the illuminated boundary length. The direct-illumination test checks if the segment from lamp to a boundary sample intersects the boundary anywhere before reaching the sample (i.e., is the point visible). For one-reflection illumination, for every boundary sample point P we reflect the lamp L across each wall AB to L', and check if the straight line from L' to P intersects AB at some Pint on the AB segment, Pint is visible from L, and the segment Pint-P lies inside the polygon. If so, P is illuminated by a single reflection. Finally, we sum the boundary length segments between consecutive samples that are lit, in order around the boundary, to estimate the total lit length; the complement is the unilluminated length. We choose a dense sampling per edge (K=60) to reduce discretization error; edge lengths and coordinates are small, so this approach should be efficient and accurate enough for the required tolerance (1e-3). The code includes numerical tolerance handling and point-in-polygon checks with boundary handling. \n\nMain steps:\n- Parse each test case.\n- Build polygon vertices in CCW order and lamp location.\n- Sample along each edge with K samples, tracking boundary position s (0..perimeter) for each sample.\n- For each sample point P, test direct visibility from lamp; if not visible, test all walls for a valid single-reflection path using the reflection trick described above.\n- Accumulate lit length by summing boundary-length deltas between consecutive samples where at least one end is lit.\n- Output the lit length; the unilluminated length is perimeter minus lit length implicitly via the sample approach.\n\nThis solution is designed to be self-contained and robust for the given constraints, and uses standard computational geometry primitives (segment intersection, point-in-polygon, reflection of a point across a line, etc.).",
